\uline{Supplement}. Return to a \uline{geometric} proof of theorem 2, corollary 1 b). We may assume that $G$ is \unsure{the} generalized Jacobian of $Y$, and it suffices to show the

\uline{Lemma} Let $Y$ be an anabelian elementary Artin variety, (over $K$ algebraically closed), $Y\hookrightarrow J_Y^1$ its embedding into its generalized Jacobian, $u\in J_Y^0(k)$ and $U$ a nonempty open subset of $Y$, such that $U+u\subset Y$. Then $u=1$\inserted{, or again: the map \unsure{$x\mapsto xu$|$x\mapsto x+u$} from $U$ to $Y$ is the identity.}
\figure{\begin{tikzpicture}\draw (0,0)--(0,2);\draw (.08,0)--(.08,2);\end{tikzpicture}}

By dévissage, we are reduced to the case where $Y$ is a curve. First suppose it complete, so that $U+u\subset Y$ implies $Y+u\subset Y$ -- then the result is well known. \unsure{This|This} follows for example from the fixed point formula, which implies that $\tau_u|_Y$ \inserted{if it is not the identity,} has $2-2g$ fixed points, which (since $g\geq2$ because of the anabelian hypothesis) is absurd.

This \deleted{means} \inserted{implies} therefore, in the general case, \inserted{at least if $g\neq1$,} that the image of $u$ in the abelian part of $J_Y^0(k)$ is zero.

\margin{If $g\geq2$, replacing $Y$ by $\widehat Y$ if necessary, we may assume $Y$ complete. If $g=1$ or 0, see homological argument below}
\margin{\unsure{serves no purpose here}}

\deleted{[Now according to the known structure of $J_Y^0$ as an extension of $J_{\widehat Y}^0$ by a torus $\mathbb G_m^I/\mathbb G_m$ (where $I=\widehat Y-Y$) $u$ is identified with an element of $(\mathbb G_m^I/\mathbb G_m)(k)$, hence is defined by $(\lambda_i)_{i\in I}$, the $\lambda_i\in k^*$, which serve to construct a line bundle}
